s rate indicates insufficient canvassing effort.\n\nOption C: \"This year most of the donations that came to Smithtown University from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university's fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors.\"\n- This option suggests that many previous donors did not receive any contact from the fund-raisers. However, it does not directly address the overall success rate or the canvassing effort. It only provides information about the behavior of previous donors, which is not directly relevant to the argument.\n\nOption D: \"Smithtown university's fund-raisers were successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before about as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people.\"\n- This option compares the success rate of Smithtown University's fund-raisers with other universities. While it provides a comparison, it does not directly support the argument about insufficient canvassing effort. It only suggests that Smithtown University's fund-raisers were not particularly successful compared to other universities in contacting new donors.\n\nAfter analyzing all the options, Option B provides the most direct support for the argument that the high success rate indicates insufficient canvassing effort. It directly addresses the behavior of the fund-raisers in contacting new donors, which is central to the argument.\n</think>\n<answer>\nB\n</answer>",
