dinary Powder Detergent in Front-Loading Washer**:\n   - **Assumption**: For a detergent to clean clothes really well in a washer, it must dissolve readily in that washer.\n   - **Reality**: Ordinary powder detergent does not dissolve readily in front-loading washers.\n   - **Outcome**: Clothes will not be cleaned really well in a front-loading washer using ordinary powder detergent.\n\n2. **Specially Formulated Detergent for Front-Loading Washer**:\n   - **Assumption**: For a detergent to clean clothes really well in a washer, it must dissolve readily in that washer.\n   - **Reality**: A detergent formulated especially for front-loading washers dissolves readily in front-loading washers.\n   - **Outcome**: Clothes will be cleaned really well in a front-loading washer using this specially formulated detergent.\n\n### Why the Assumption is Necessary:\nThe assumption \"For a detergent to clean clothes really well in a washer, it must dissolve readily in that washer\" is necessary because it directly connects the dissolving property of the detergent to its effectiveness in cleaning clothes. Without this assumption, the argument that using a detergent formulated especially for front-loading washers is necessary to get clothes really clean in front-loading machines would be weakened.\n\n### Example Illustration:\n- **Without the Assumption**: If the assumption were not true, it would be possible to use ordinary powder detergent in a front-loading washer and still get clothes really clean. This would contradict the argument that ordinary powder detergent does not dissolve readily in front-loading washers and that a specially formulated detergent is needed.\n- **With the Assumption**: The assumption ensures that the dissolving property of the detergent is a critical factor. Therefore, if ordinary powder detergent does not dissolve readily in front-loading washers, it cannot clean clothes really well, necessitating the use of a specially formulated detergent.\n\nThus, the a